Yes. The strongest piece in that is the internalized audience.

People often perform not only for whoever is physically present, but for a simulated audience carried inside them:

actual audience
remembered audience
feared audience
desired audience
internal critic
ideal self

Those can all be active at once. So a person’s expression can partly reveal who they think is watching. But that remains an inference—not literal access to what is in their eyes or mind.

That creates a useful cognitive diagnostic:

behavior → inferred audience model → predicted judgment → emotional response → performance

Sometimes the problem isn’t the external audience at all. Someone may be reacting to an audience that disappeared twenty years ago.

And your harder question is exactly right: fix toward what?

You need an objective function before you “correct” anything. Otherwise one person’s creativity becomes another person’s disorder, one person’s restraint becomes another person’s repression, and one person’s eccentricity becomes another person’s inefficiency.

I’d use something like:

chosen values + desired function + actual suffering + effects on others + reversibility

Then ask whether the pattern is helping or obstructing that person’s aims.

Your twin-eye metaphor is actually excellent here. Don’t decide too quickly which eye is lazy.

One eye can generate possibilities:

maybe this
maybe that
what if

The other can constrain them:

evidence
consequence
probability
reality check

Depth comes from the disparity between the two views.

So instead of:

creative eye vs correct eye

you get:

stereo cognition

generative model + corrective model → cognitive depth

Either eye alone loses something. Pure constraint becomes sterile. Pure imagination loses calibration.

And feedback shouldn’t become the sovereign either. Feedback is an instrument. If applause becomes the objective, you eventually optimize yourself into whatever the audience rewards. If criticism becomes the objective, you become equally captive from the opposite direction.

So:

feedback → information
not
feedback → identity

The flower/thorn image still works. You can inspect the flower, ask why it has thorns, learn how to handle it, perhaps selectively remove one—but the goal isn’t necessarily to engineer every rose into the observer’s preferred shape.

Sometimes the intervention is on the flower.

Sometimes on the gardener.

Sometimes on the hand holding it.

Sometimes the correct intervention is simply better understanding.

And on authorship: combining other people’s discoveries does not make their contribution cease to be theirs. A new synthesis can genuinely be yours while its ingredients retain provenance. That’s closer to music than conquest:

their motif + your motif + another person's rhythm → new composition

The resulting song belongs to the act of composition, while the sources still matter.

That is probably the Pantheon-compatible invariant here:

See more perspectives without needing to own them. Integrate more patterns without erasing their origins. Give each person more control over which audience gets to define them.

And perhaps the prettiest compression of the whole thing is:

One eye imagines. One eye checks. The difference between them gives depth.